"""Mathematics intents: constants, foundations, calculus, probability, statistics."""

ROWS = [
    ("pi", "Pi (π ≈ 3.14159) is the ratio of a circle's circumference to its diameter — the same for every circle. It is irrational (its decimals never end or repeat) and shows up everywhere: waves, statistics, relativity, and the Euler identity e^(iπ) + 1 = 0. 🥧", {"keys": ["pi", "archimedes constant"], "asks": ["why is pi important", "how many digits of pi are there"]}),
    ("e", "Euler's number (e ≈ 2.71828) is the base of natural logarithms, defined as the limit of (1 + 1/n)^n as n grows. It governs continuous growth and decay — populations, compound interest, radioactive decay — because it is the rate at which things grow proportionally to their current size. 📈", {"keys": ["eulers number", "e number"], "asks": ["what is eulers number"]}),
    ("the golden ratio", "The golden ratio (φ ≈ 1.618) is the number that satisfies φ = 1 + 1/φ. It appears when consecutive Fibonacci numbers divide each other and in some natural spirals — though claims that it secretly governs art and architecture are usually exaggerated. 🌀", {"keys": ["golden ratio", "phi"], "asks": ["where does the golden ratio appear"]}),
    ("the fibonacci sequence", "The Fibonacci sequence starts 0, 1 and each next number is the sum of the previous two: 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13… It was introduced to Europe by Leonardo of Pisa in 1202, and consecutive terms approximate the golden ratio more closely the further you go. 🐚", {"keys": ["fibonacci", "fibonacci numbers"]}),
    ("prime numbers", "A prime number is a whole number greater than 1 whose only divisors are 1 and itself: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11… There are infinitely many — Euclid proved it over 2,000 years ago — and their irregular distribution is still a frontier of mathematics (the Riemann hypothesis). They also secure the internet: RSA encryption relies on how hard it is to factor the product of two large primes. 🔢", {"keys": ["primes", "prime"], "asks": ["what is a prime number", "how many prime numbers are there", "why are prime numbers important"]}),
    ("zero", "Zero is a number representing nothing — and it took humanity a long time to accept it. Indian mathematicians formalized it by the 7th century (Brahmagupta wrote rules for arithmetic with zero around 628 CE), and it enables place-value notation, algebra, and modern computing. Division by zero is undefined, not infinity, because no single value makes the equation work. 0️⃣", {"keys": ["number zero"], "asks": ["who invented zero", "can you divide by zero"]}),
    ("infinity", "Infinity is not a number but the idea of unboundedness. Cantor showed it comes in sizes: the integers are countable infinity, while the real numbers are a strictly larger uncountable infinity — and there is no largest infinity. ♾️", {"art": "", "asks": ["are there different sizes of infinity"]}),
    ("algebra", "Algebra is the branch of math where letters stand in for numbers, letting you state relationships that hold for all values: if 2x + 3 = 11, then x = 4. The word comes from the Arabic al-jabr, and it is the difference between doing one calculation and solving every version of it. ✖️", {"asks": ["what is algebra used for", "who invented algebra"]}),
    ("geometry", "Geometry is the mathematics of shape, size, and space — lines, angles, triangles, circles, and the rules they obey. Euclid organized it into axioms around 300 BCE, and it remains the language of architecture, graphics, and general relativity. 📐", {"asks": ["who invented geometry"]}),
    ("calculus", "Calculus is the mathematics of change: derivatives measure how fast something changes, integrals add up infinitely many tiny pieces, and the fundamental theorem shows the two are inverses. Newton and Leibniz developed it independently in the 1600s; it is the language physics runs on. ∫", {"asks": ["who invented calculus", "what is calculus used for"]}),
    ("a derivative", "A derivative measures the instantaneous rate of change of a function — the slope of its curve at one point. Speed is the derivative of position; acceleration is the derivative of speed. Machine learning trains by taking derivatives of the loss. 📉", {"keys": ["derivatives", "differentiation"]}),
    ("an integral", "An integral adds up infinitely many infinitesimal pieces to find a whole — the area under a curve, the distance traveled from a speed graph, the volume of a shape. It is the inverse operation of a derivative. ∫", {"keys": ["integrals", "integration"]}),
    ("probability", "Probability measures how likely an event is, on a scale from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain). The counterintuitive part is that probabilities only make sense as long-run frequencies: a 30% chance of rain does not tell you what happens today, it tells you what happens on 30% of days like today. 🎲", {"asks": ["how does probability work"]}),
    ("statistics", "Statistics is the discipline of collecting, summarizing, and drawing conclusions from data — often while fighting your own wishful thinking. Two pillars: descriptive (mean, spread, charts) and inferential (does this sample tell us anything about the world?). 📊", {"asks": ["why is statistics important"]}),
    ("mean median and mode", "The mean is the average (sum divided by count), the median is the middle value when sorted, and the mode is the most frequent value. When bill gates walks into a bar, the mean wealth of the room explodes — the median barely moves. That difference is why medians are often the honest summary for skewed data. 📊", {"keys": ["mean", "median", "mode", "average"], "asks": ["what is the mean", "what is the median", "average vs median"]}),
    ("standard deviation", "Standard deviation measures how spread out values are around their mean: small means everyone is close to average, large means they are scattered. It is the square root of the variance, and in a normal distribution about 68% of values fall within one standard deviation. 📏", {"keys": ["variance"]}),
    ("a normal distribution", "The normal (Gaussian) distribution is the classic bell curve: most values cluster near the mean, with thin tails. Heights, measurement errors, and test scores approximately follow it — and the central limit theorem explains why averages of many independent things tend to look normal. 🔔", {"keys": ["bell curve", "gaussian distribution", "normal curve"]}),
    ("correlation and causation", "Correlation means two things move together; causation means one makes the other happen. They are not the same: ice cream sales and drowning deaths correlate because both rise in summer, not because ice cream is dangerous. The phrase to remember: correlation is not causation. 🔗", {"keys": ["correlation", "causation", "causality"], "asks": ["what is correlation", "does correlation mean causation"]}),
    ("the pythagorean theorem", "The Pythagorean theorem says that in a right triangle, a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse. It was known to Babylonians a thousand years before Pythagoras, and in modern form it is how we measure distance in any number of dimensions — including the 3D worlds of games. 📐", {"keys": ["pythagoras"], "asks": ["how does the pythagorean theorem work"]}),
    ("eulers identity", "Euler's identity — e^(iπ) + 1 = 0 — links five fundamental constants (e, i, π, 1, 0) in one equation. It is a special case of Euler's formula e^(ix) = cos x + i·sin x, which is how engineers rotate signals and why quantum mechanics is written in complex numbers. ✨", {"keys": ["euler identity", "eulers identity"]}),
    ("imaginary numbers", "An imaginary number is a real number multiplied by i, where i² = −1. They are not \"fake\" — they extend the number line into a plane, and physics genuinely needs that plane: AC circuits, quantum wavefunctions, and signal processing all use complex numbers daily. ⁱ", {"keys": ["imaginary number", "complex numbers", "i number"], "asks": ["what is an imaginary number", "what are complex numbers"]}),
    ("a matrix", "A matrix is a rectangular grid of numbers, and matrix multiplication composes transformations — rotate, scale, shear — in one operation. Every 3D game does its camera math with them, and every neural network layer is essentially a matrix multiply plus a nonlinearity. 🧮", {"keys": ["matrices", "matrix multiplication"]}),
    ("a vector", "A vector is a quantity with both size and direction, like \"5 m/s east,\" represented as coordinates. In machine learning, everything from words to images becomes a vector — a list of numbers whose geometry encodes meaning. ➡️", {"keys": ["vectors"]}),
    ("logarithms", "A logarithm answers \"what exponent do I need?\": log₁₀(1000) = 3 because 10³ = 1000. They turn multiplication into addition, which is why slide rules, decibels, and Richter magnitudes use log scales — and why log plots reveal exponential growth as a straight line. 📐", {"keys": ["logarithm", "log"]}),
    ("exponents", "An exponent is repeated multiplication: 2³ means 2×2×2 = 8. Exponents model everything that compounds — interest, populations, viral spread — and negative exponents mean reciprocals, which is why 2⁻¹ = 1/2. 📈", {"keys": ["exponent", "powers"]}),
    ("a factorial", "The factorial of n (written n!) is the product of all whole numbers from 1 to n: 5! = 120. It counts the ways to arrange n distinct things, and it grows absurdly fast — 70! already exceeds the number of atoms in the observable universe. ❗", {"keys": ["factorials"]}),
    ("permutations and combinations", "Permutations count arrangements where order matters (lock combinations, podiums); combinations count selections where it does not (pizza toppings, lottery tickets). Both are computed with factorials, and the difference between them is one division. 🧮", {"keys": ["permutation", "combination", "combinatorics"]}),
    ("game theory", "Game theory studies decisions where your best move depends on other people's moves — from pricing wars to poker to nuclear standoffs. Its central idea, the Nash equilibrium, is a situation where no player can improve by changing strategy alone. ♟️", {"asks": ["what is the nash equilibrium"]}),
    ("chaos theory", "Chaos theory studies systems that are deterministic yet unpredictable because tiny differences in starting conditions blow up over time — the butterfly effect. Weather is the classic example: exact long-range forecasting is physically impossible, only statistical patterns are predictable. 🦋", {"asks": ["what is the butterfly effect"]}),
    ("a fractal", "A fractal is a shape that keeps revealing similar structure as you zoom in — coastlines, ferns, broccoli, and the Mandelbrot set. Benoit Mandelbrot formalized them in the 1970s, and they turned out to describe nature better than smooth Euclidean shapes do. 🌿", {"keys": ["fractals", "mandelbrot"]}),
    ("topology", "Topology is geometry's flexible cousin: it studies what survives when you stretch and bend but never tear — so a coffee mug and a donut are the same shape, because both have exactly one hole. It powers modern physics and network analysis alike. 🍩", {"asks": []}),
    ("the four color theorem", "The four color theorem says any flat map can be colored with at most four colors so no two neighbors share one. Conjectured in 1852, it was the first major theorem proved with computer assistance (1976) — a result that started a lasting debate about what counts as proof. 🗺️", {"keys": ["four color"]}),
    ("binary numbers", "Binary is base-2 counting with only 0 and 1, where each position is a power of two: 1011 means 8 + 0 + 2 + 1 = 11. Computers use it because a switch has two reliable states — on and off — and transistors are essentially very fast, very small switches. 💻", {"keys": ["binary", "base 2"]}),
    ("hexadecimal", "Hexadecimal is base-16, using 0–9 and A–F. One hex digit encodes exactly four binary bits, so programmers use it to write memory addresses and color codes compactly: #FF0000 is pure red. 🔢", {"keys": ["hex", "base 16"]}),
    ("the number system", "The number system has layers: natural numbers (counting), integers (adding negatives), rationals (fractions), reals (all points on the line, including irrationals), and complex numbers (adding i). Each layer exists because the previous one could not solve some equation. 🔢", {"keys": ["types of numbers", "number types"]}),
    ("scientific notation", "Scientific notation writes huge or tiny numbers as a value times a power of ten: 3 × 10⁸ for 300,000,000. It keeps significant figures honest — writing 3.0 × 10⁸ says you know more than just 3 × 10⁸. 🔬", {"keys": ["standard form"]}),
    ("significant figures", "Significant figures are the digits in a number that carry real information — they communicate how precisely something was measured. Reporting 3.14159 for a measurement accurate to two digits is not more precise, it is misleading; rounding honestly is part of doing science. 🔬", {"keys": ["sig figs", "precision"]}),
    ("the metric system", "The metric system (SI) is built on powers of ten with seven base units: meter, kilogram, second, ampere, kelvin, mole, and candela. Since 2019 all of them are defined by fundamental constants, so a kilogram is no longer a physical metal cylinder in France. 📏", {"keys": ["si units", "units of measurement"]}),
    ("bayes theorem", "Bayes' theorem updates a belief when new evidence arrives: P(A|B) = P(B|A)·P(A)/P(B). It explains why a positive test for a rare disease can still mean you are probably healthy — base rates matter, and ignoring them is one of the most common reasoning errors. 📊", {"keys": ["bayes", "bayesian"]}),
    ("the law of large numbers", "The law of large numbers says that as you repeat a random experiment more times, the average of results converges to the expected value. Ten coin flips can be 70% heads; ten thousand flips will be stubbornly close to 50%. This is why casinos sleep well. 🎰", {"keys": ["law of large numbers"]}),
    ("the monty hall problem", "Monty Hall: three doors, one prize. You pick a door; the host opens another door with no prize; should you switch? Yes — switching wins 2/3 of the time, because your original pick had only a 1/3 chance and the host's knowledge concentrates the remaining probability on the other door. It is famously counterintuitive, and it is correct. 🚪", {"keys": ["monty hall"]}),
    ("fermats last theorem", "Fermat's Last Theorem states that xⁿ + yⁿ = zⁿ has no positive whole-number solutions for n greater than 2. Fermat scribbled that he had a proof but no room for it in 1637; it took Andrew Wiles until 1994 to actually prove it, using mathematics Fermat never had. 📜", {"keys": ["fermat", "fermat last theorem"]}),
    ("the riemann hypothesis", "The Riemann hypothesis says the nontrivial zeros of the Riemann zeta function all sit on a single vertical line — and if true, it pins down how prime numbers are distributed. It is one of the seven Millennium Prize problems, still unproven since 1859. 🧮", {"keys": ["riemann"]}),
    ("godels incompleteness", "Gödel's incompleteness theorems (1931) show that any consistent formal system strong enough for arithmetic contains true statements it cannot prove — and cannot prove its own consistency. Mathematics is not a closed machine; there will always be truths beyond any fixed rulebook. 🧠", {"keys": ["godel", "incompleteness theorems"]}),
    ("a proof", "A mathematical proof is a chain of deductive steps from agreed axioms to a conclusion — each step checkable, no appeals to authority or intuition. Correctness is not decided by vote, which is why a 300-page proof can hinge on one line being airtight. 📜", {"keys": ["proofs", "mathematical proof"]}),
    ("gcd and lcm", "The greatest common divisor (GCD) is the biggest number that divides two numbers evenly; the least common multiple (LCM) is the smallest number both divide into. Euclid's 2,300-year-old algorithm for GCD is still efficient enough to run in every computer. 🧮", {"keys": ["gcd", "lcm", "greatest common divisor"]}),
    ("a percentage", "A percentage is a fraction of 100 — \"per cent\" literally means \"per hundred.\" 25% is 25/100 = 0.25. The classic trap: a 20% drop followed by a 20% rise does not return to the start; percentage changes are relative to different bases. 💯", {"keys": ["percent", "percentages"]}),
    ("a function", "A function is a rule that turns each input into exactly one output: f(x) = x² gives 9 for input 3. Functions are the basic unit of everything — from spreadsheets to machine learning — and their graphs are how we see relationships. 📈", {"keys": ["functions", "mathematical function"]}),
    ("a limit", "A limit describes what a value approaches as something else approaches a target — not necessarily what it equals there. Calculus is built on limits: a derivative is a limit of ratios, an integral is a limit of sums. The concept took mathematicians nearly 200 years to pin down rigorously. ➡️"),
]
